      "id": "18433",
      "key": "SFCC-237",
      "summary": "Spike: MRT Application Architecture Strategy (Single vs Per-Integration MRT Apps)",
      "description": "Objective\n\n\nProduce a TSD comparing two approaches for structuring the storefront's MRT (Managed Runtime) Node layer as integrations are built out — \nOption A:\n a single MRT application responsible for all integrations, vs \nOption B:\n a dedicated MRT application per integration/domain — so the squad makes a deliberate, signed-off decision on deployment topology before the integration build-out hardens it.\nBackground\n\n\nThe storefront currently runs as a single PWA Kit application on MRT (the PWA Kit default), which today isolates upstream integrations only at the network level (per-origin \nundici\n connection pools). As integration work begins (~18 systems, nearly all not yet started), the number of MRT applications — and how integration/domain code is split across them — becomes a hard-to-retrofit decision affecting deploy/rollback, failure isolation, secrets, cost, and ownership. Tim has asked Angelo (FED) and Gideon (SFCC) to collaborate on this. Complements SFCC-122 (which inventoried what MRT can \nconsume\n); this determines how many MRT apps we \ndeploy\n and how integration work is split across them. The as-is baseline is to be confirmed with Gideon / platform before the options analysis.\nTasks\nConfirm the current-state (as-is) baseline with Gideon/platform: single MRT app, proxies wired today, in-process isolation model, the cartridge vs MRT-mediated vs SFCC→MuleSoft integration boundary, deploy/secret model, and platform limits.\nConfirm whether MRT supports multiple independent applications/projects in our org, and the cost model per environment/app.\nDefine and compare the options — Option A (single app), Option B (per-integration/domain app), and any viable middle option (single modular app) — across deploy/rollback, failure isolation (event loop / memory / process, not just sockets), secrets & security blast radius, performance/LCP, cost, and ownership.\nAssess the MRT execution/concurrency model and its effect on failure blast radius.\nRecommend an option with rationale tied to Zimmermann constraints (LCP ≤ 2s, fail-independently, multi-region, squad ownership).\nCapture the decision in a TSD using the team template; secure Technical Lead sign-off.\nDefinition of Done\n\n\nA TSD comparing Option A vs Option B (and any middle option), with an agreed as-is baseline, a recommended option and rationale, and Technical Lead sign-off — sufficient to set the MRT application topology before integration build-out begins.",
