    {
      "id": "18389",
      "key": "SFCC-205",
      "summary": "Spike: Figma to React Setup",
      "description": "Objective\nRefine, improve, and harden the Figma-to-code handover workflow from the design team, so that what reaches engineering (specs, tokens, the component contract, and prototypes) is unambiguous, verifiable, and ready to build against for the Zimmermann storefront. Building on the findings of the confirmatory handover audit already completed, agree the workflow changes required to tighten the design-to-build handoff.\nBackground\nThe Zimmermann design deliverables are produced through the DDP Design OS repository: design tokens, a component contract, per-component and per-screen specs, and interactive prototypes. A confirmatory audit of the current handover has been completed and identified a number of gaps that make parts of the deliverable ambiguous or hard to build against: inconsistent responsive capture, a missing handover quality gate, source-of-truth and provenance gaps, incomplete spec coverage, and accessibility requirements that are asserted but not decomposed into checkable items. This spike reviews those findings and agrees the workflow changes needed to close them, so future handovers arrive build-ready. Time-boxed investigation; the output is documentation, not build work.\nTasks\nReview the findings of the completed handover audit and confirm the priority workflow gaps to address.\nDefine a \"ready for build\" acceptance standard for each handover artefact (specs, tokens, component contract, prototypes).\nAgree a handover quality gate (coverage, source fidelity, completeness, accessibility) that must pass before a deliverable is marked ready for build.\nAgree how responsive intent is captured and expressed in the handover (fluid values, breakpoints, layout direction) rather than fixed single-viewport snapshots.\nAgree the source-of-truth and provenance approach: which design source is authoritative, how it is verified, and how each artefact traces back to it.\nConfirm accessibility requirements (WCAG AA) are decomposed into checkable items carried through the handover.\nLog the agreed remediation actions and owners; raise follow-on stories where build or tooling work is required.\nDefinition of Done\nThe priority gaps from the handover audit are confirmed and documented.\nA \"ready for build\" acceptance standard is agreed for each handover artefact (specs, tokens, component contract, prototypes).\nA handover quality gate is defined (what must pass before \"ready for build\"), covering coverage, source fidelity, completeness, and accessibility.\nThe approach to responsive capture, source-of-truth / provenance, and accessibility is agreed and documented.\nRemediation actions are logged with owners, and follow-on stories are raised where build or tooling work is required.\nThe hardened workflow is documented and shared with the squad.",
      "status": {
        "name": "Done",
        "category": "Done",
        "color": "green"
      },
